counterfactuals.output:
A method of forming conductive bumps on opposing surfaces of a substrate, comprising:

forming a first-side under bump metallization (UBM) layer over a first surface of the substrate;

forming a second-side UBM layer over a second surface of the substrate opposite the first surface;

forming a plurality of first metal bumps on selected portions of the first-side UBM layer;

forming a plurality of second metal bumps on selected portions of the second-side UBM layer;

removing exposed portions of the first-side UBM layer and the second-side UBM layer not covered by the first metal bumps and the second metal bumps, respectively; and

reflowing the first metal bumps and the second metal bumps to form reflowed conductive bumps on both the first and second surfaces of the substrate.

A method of forming interconnect structures on a semiconductor substrate, comprising:

depositing an under-bump metallization layer on a first side of the semiconductor substrate;

forming a first plurality of bump structures on the under-bump metallization layer on the first side;

depositing an under-bump metallization layer on a second side of the semiconductor substrate opposite the first side;

forming a second plurality of bump structures on the under-bump metallization layer on the second side;

removing exposed portions of the under-bump metallization layers from the first and second sides of the semiconductor substrate, wherein portions of the under-bump metallization layers underlying the first and second plurality of bump structures remain; and

subsequently reflowing the first and second plurality of bump structures.
